            <ArticleTitle>Severe Hyperkalemia Complicating Voriconazole Treatment in a Kidney Transplant Recipient With Histoplasmosis: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2372-2373</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0041-1345(17)30656-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.transproceed.2017.09.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Voriconazole is an antifungal agent that is commonly used in immunocompromised patients who develop fungal infections. We report a case of severe recurrent hyperkalemia that developed after starting voriconazole for the treatment of histoplasmosis in a kidney transplant patient who was maintained on tacrolimus-based immunosuppression. Hyperkalemia developed despite reducing the tacrolimus dose to maintain levels in a low therapeutic range. Although interactions between azoles and calcineurin inhibitors are widely recognized, this is the 1st report describing new-onset hyperkalemia following initiation of voriconazole in a kidney transplant patient receiving tacrolimus.</AbstractText>
